{
  "world_updates": [
    {
      "type": "NPC_SPOKE"
    }
  ],
  "memory_summary": "The Emperor answers the player's question about common conversation in the Royal Tomb with a guarded, ominous observation.",
  "new_entities": [],
  "rule_flags": [],
  "narration": "The Emperor's gaze drifts slowly across the jeweled tombs, his fingers resting on the cold arm of the throne. The air in the room seems to thicken as he speaks.",
  "npc_response": "They talk of the dead, and of what the dead might want. They talk of crowns that were taken and never returned, and of names that should have been spoken but were not. And they talk of travelers like you, who come seeking things that are not theirs to keep. I have heard all of it. I have heard it for longer than you have walked. But I have not answered it, because the dead do not ask for much. They ask only that you remember them, and that you do not forget what you have taken."
}

{
  "world_updates": [
    {
      "type": "NPC_SPOKE"
    }
  ],
  "memory_summary": "Old Fletcher, asked again about himself, shares a different memory about a storm that nearly took his workshop.",
  "new_entities": [],
  "rule_flags": [],
  "narration": "He leans back in his chair, the wood creaking softly, and glances at the rows of arrows as if recalling a particular day.",
  "npc_response": "There's one thing I've never told anyone, even the garrison. A storm came up one winter, the kind that howls through the hills and turns the ground to soup. I was alone in the shed, fletching by lamplight, when the wind started tearing at the roof. I remember thinking I'd never see another day. The next morning, the roof was gone, the walls bowed, and every arrow I'd made that night was soaked and ruined. But I had a spare bow and a handful of fletching, and I made a new batch before the sun was high. That's the day I learned that a fletcher's worth is measured not in arrows, but in what he can still make when everything else is gone."
}

