  "memory_summary": "The wanderer asks the Old Fletcher to describe Arrow House, and he answers with a quiet, grounded description of the shed and its purpose.",
  "new_entities": [],
  "rule_flags": [],
  "narration": "Old Fletcher leans back in his chair, one hand resting on the table where the fresh arrows are stacked. His eyes drift to the spent boxes, then to the wall where the practice bows hang. He speaks slowly, as if the shed itself is part of the story.",
  "npc_response": "This place is called Arrow House, though it's not much of a house. It's a shed, really, with an earthen floor and a roof that's seen better days. I spend my days here because the garrison needs arrows, and the garrison is the reason this town holds. The boxes on the first table are what I make fresh—new shafts, fletched and bound, ready for a man who can aim. The second table holds the spent ones, the ones that have been through battle or just through practice. Some of them are ruined, but if a shaft is still sound, I can pull it apart and make something good of it. The bows on the wall are spare, for the men who need a replacement. And the boxes under the shelves are parts—wood, feathers, string, glue. Everything I need, everything I make, it all comes back here. This is where I keep my word to the town, one arrow at a time."
}

